### What is the Facade pattern?

Facade is about simplification and decoupling. You have a subsystem of many classes that must be used together in a particular sequence. A Facade is a single class that exposes a small number of high-level methods and orchestrates the subsystem internally.

```java
package com.bank.facade;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountOpeningFacade {
    private final KycService kyc;
    private final AccountRepository repo;
    private final CardIssuer cards;
    private final NotificationService notifier;

    public AccountOpeningFacade(KycService kyc, AccountRepository repo,
                                CardIssuer cards, NotificationService notifier) {
        this.kyc = kyc; this.repo = repo;
        this.cards = cards; this.notifier = notifier;
    }

    public String openAccount(AccountRequest request) {
        kyc.verify(request.getPan());
        String accountId = repo.create(request);
        cards.issueDebitCard(accountId);
        notifier.sendWelcome(request.getEmail());
        return accountId;
    }
}
```

The client calls one method instead of coordinating four services.

### What is the Adapter pattern?

Adapter is about compatibility. You have an existing class whose interface does not match what your code expects. The Adapter sits between them and translates.

```java
package com.bank.adapter;

// Target interface the bank's code expects
public interface CreditScoreProvider {
    int getScore(String pan);
}

// Third-party vendor with incompatible interface
public class CibilApi {
    public CibilResponse fetch(CibilRequest request) {
        // vendor-specific implementation
        return new CibilResponse(750);
    }
}

// Adapter translates vendor to target
public class CibilAdapter implements CreditScoreProvider {
    private final CibilApi api;

    public CibilAdapter(CibilApi api) {
        this.api = api;
    }

    @Override
    public int getScore(String pan) {
        CibilRequest request = new CibilRequest(pan);
        CibilResponse response = api.fetch(request);
        return response.getScore();
    }
}
```

### How do Facade and Adapter differ?

The core difference is intent.

| Aspect | Facade | Adapter |
|---|---|---|
| Intent | Simplify a complex subsystem | Bridge an interface mismatch |
| Number of objects | Fronts many subsystem classes | Wraps a single adaptee |
| Interface design | You design a new convenient interface | Target interface already exists and is fixed |
| When to use | Proactive: subsystem is painful | Reactive: discovered a mismatch |
| Scope | Many services orchestrated | One class translated |
| Client knowledge | Client never knows the subsystem | Client never knows the adaptee |

```java
// Facade: simplifies many things into one call
accountOpeningFacade.openAccount(request);

// Adapter: translates one thing into the expected interface
CreditScoreProvider provider = new CibilAdapter(new CibilApi());
int score = provider.getScore("ABCDE1234F");
```

### When do you use Facade vs Adapter?

Use Facade when a subsystem has many classes that must be used together and the calling code is getting complex. You want one clean entry point. Use Adapter when you must integrate a library, legacy system, or vendor whose interface does not match what your code expects and you cannot or do not want to change it.

```java
// Facade scenario: opening an account requires 4 steps
// Without facade, the controller does this:
kyc.verify(pan);
String id = repo.create(request);
cards.issueDebitCard(id);
notifier.sendWelcome(email);
// With facade, the controller does this:
facade.openAccount(request); // one call

// Adapter scenario: vendor API has different method signature
// Without adapter, your code is coupled to the vendor:
CibilResponse resp = cibilApi.fetch(new CibilRequest(pan));
int score = resp.getScore();
// With adapter, your code uses your own interface:
int score = provider.getScore(pan);
```

### Can Facade and Adapter coexist?

Yes. A Facade can internally use Adapters to talk to incompatible subsystem pieces. This is common in real systems.

```java
package com.bank.facade;

import com.bank.adapter.CibilAdapter;
import com.bank.adapter.CibilApi;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LoanApprovalFacade {
    private final CreditScoreProvider scoreProvider; // Adapter
    private final LoanRepository loanRepo;
    private final NotificationService notifier;

    public LoanApprovalFacade() {
        // Facade uses an Adapter internally
        this.scoreProvider = new CibilAdapter(new CibilApi());
        this.loanRepo = new LoanRepository();
        this.notifier = new NotificationService();
    }

    public boolean approveLoan(String pan, BigDecimal amount) {
        int score = scoreProvider.getScore(pan); // via Adapter
        if (score < 650) return false;
        loanRepo.create(pan, amount);
        notifier.sendApproval(pan);
        return true;
    }
}
```

### What are the object adapter and class adapter forms?

An **object adapter** holds a reference to the adaptee and delegates, which is the common composition-based form. A **class adapter** uses multiple inheritance (in Java: implementing the target interface while extending the adaptee), which is less flexible.

```java
// Object adapter (preferred): composition
public class CibilAdapter implements CreditScoreProvider {
    private final CibilApi api; // holds reference
    // ...
}

// Class adapter: inheritance (less flexible)
public class CibilClassAdapter extends CibilApi implements CreditScoreProvider {
    @Override
    public int getScore(String pan) {
        return this.fetch(new CibilRequest(pan)).getScore();
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is confusing Facade with Adapter because both wrap other objects. The quick test: am I simplifying many things into one easy interface (Facade), or am I translating one specific thing into a different expected interface (Adapter)?

```java
// Wrong mental model: "both are wrappers, so they are the same"
// Correct mental model:
// Facade = one simple door to a complex building
// Adapter = one plug converter for one incompatible device
```

### What are real Java examples?

`java.util.Arrays.asList` adapts an array to the `List` interface: that is an Adapter. SLF4J bindings adapt one logging API to another: that is an Adapter. A service class that wraps several repositories and clients to offer one business operation is a Facade.

```java
// Adapter: Arrays.asList converts array to List interface
List<String> list = Arrays.asList("HDFC", "ICICI", "SBI");

// Facade: service wraps multiple collaborators
transferFacade.transfer(fromId, toId, amount);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer recognises that a Facade is proactive simplification planned during design, while an Adapter is often reactive glue to bridge a mismatch discovered during integration. Both are complementary. At National Bank of India, the account-opening flow uses a Facade to orchestrate KYC, account creation, card issuance, and notification, and inside that Facade uses a `CibilAdapter` to bridge the vendor's credit-score API to the bank's internal `CreditScoreProvider` interface.
